mm_translocation_dictionary.tsv — partner naming note
=====================================================

Partner names in this file (partner_a / partner_b) use the dictionary's own
convention, which differs from the panel BED labels on purpose:

  - Ig loci carry a "_locus" suffix here (IGH_locus, IGK_locus, IGL_locus)
    but are labeled plainly in the BED (IGH, IGK, IGL / IGL/IGLL5).
  - Compound partners are joined with "_" here (FGFR3_NSD2, WWOX_MAF,
    FCRL4_FCRL5) but with "/" in the BED (FGFR3/NSD2, WWOX/MAF, FCRL5/FCRL4).

These names are NOT compared to the BED as exact strings. The annotation
matches on gene identity: each partner name is reduced to a set of gene
tokens (split on "/" and "_", uppercased, with region-suffix words like
"locus" dropped), and two partners match when their token sets intersect.
This is implemented in bin/annotate_mm_translocations.py (_norm_tokens and
dictionary_lookup).

Consequences worth knowing when editing this file:
  - Order within a pair does not matter (IGH_locus/CCND1 == CCND1/IGH_locus).
  - Order of the two components in a compound does not matter
    (FCRL4_FCRL5 == FCRL5/FCRL4).
  - A BED region that carries an extra component the dictionary does not name
    still matches (BED "IGL/IGLL5" matches dictionary "IGL_locus" on IGL).
  - Because matching is token-based, keep partner names as real gene symbols.
    Do not invent joined tokens that are not genes; the only special word the
    normalizer strips is "locus".

To add a new canonical pair, add a row with partner_a / partner_b as gene
symbols (either naming convention works) plus name / frequency / references /
notes. No code change is needed.


Columns added at v0.4.0
=======================

tier            How firmly the pair is established, and as what kind of event.

                  defining   a primary IGH translocation. An initiating
                             event, mutually exclusive with hyperdiploidy.
                  secondary  a MYC rearrangement. A progression event, and
                             graded separately for that reason rather than
                             being ranked below the primaries by frequency.
                  emerging   published but rare, or a single-report partner.

                A tier is what makes a row survive the report's SV-type
                filter and earn an IGV page whatever its SV type. Leave it
                empty for a pair you do not want graded.

                The annotator appends "?" when the match was only partial,
                so a graded row always states how firmly it was graded.

entity          The entity the pair implies, written out. Rendered in the
                report beside the pair name.

partner_b_band  The cytoband where partner_b is expected, for a partner the
                panel does not capture. When one side matches a gene and the
                other resolves only to a cytoband, this is what still names
                the event. Every partner in this file is currently on-panel,
                so the column is empty throughout; it exists so a partner can
                be added here without also being added to the panel.

min_span_bp     Minimum span for an intrachromosomal event to keep this
                pair's entity claim. Guards against a small intra-interval
                event matching on tokens alone when both partners share one
                panel interval. No row needs it at present.

Grading is a biological prior and belongs here, in the dictionary, not in
the annotator. bin/annotate_mm_translocations.py contains no pair, tier or
entity of its own.
